    {
     "id": "editorial",
     "visible": true,
     "kicker": "dossier-process · ARGUED",
     "title": "Process — What an Achieved Form Makes Possible Next",
     "italic": "Paraphrased bounded source passages; Argued native coordination; Offered technical tests",
     "body": "",
     "x": 0.033,
     "y": 0.05,
     "width": 470,
     "size": 25,
     "align": "left"
    },
    {
     "id": "body",
     "visible": true,
     "kicker": "",
     "title": "",
     "italic": "",
     "body": "A process account must show how a determination takes form and what its occurrence changes. Its product is available for another act: a memory bears upon an encounter, a rule constrains a decision, a response changes a relationship, and a tool alters the possible course of work. A14, Computational Process Ontology grounds this native claim through the distinction between operative grammar, epistemic inspection and ontology.",
     "x": 0.033,
